WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.460
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.500
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.320
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.180
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.460
by giving you the facts.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.560
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.620
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.200
Today we conclude our series

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.140
on how industries impact
dietary and health guidelines.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.440
Did you know that the
meat industry’s own study

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.500
concluded that meat consumption
increased the risk of cancer,

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.440
heart disease, diabetes,
and premature death.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.820
Here’s the story.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.420
A series of papers published
in the Annals of Internal Medicine

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:40.380
that largely discounted all but the
highest quality randomized studies

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:44.460
reached a conclusion directly
contrary to the public health advice

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.520
we've heard for years. They
suggested that we should

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.480
continue our current consumption
of both red and processed meat.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.960
The authors based their exclusion
of evidence on the so-called GRADE

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.340
criteria, which were mainly developed
for evaluating evidence from drug trials.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.480
We need randomized double-blind,
placebo-controlled trials for drugs,

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.260
but strictness of these criteria would
probably cause evidence for just about

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.700
every dietary, lifestyle, and
environmental factor related

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.200
to chronic disease to be graded
as "low" or "very low" evidence.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.620
If the GRADE criteria were used
to evaluate the evidence

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.860
for other factors related to diet,
such as inadequate fruits

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.840
and vegetables or too
much soda or alcohol,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.740
or whether or not exercise
is good, or safe sex,

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.600
or sleep, smoking, air pollution,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.380
none of the current recommendations
on these issues would be supported

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.780
by high- or even moderate-quality
evidence using the drug trial criteria.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:43.260
But even after ignoring major
parts of the available evidence,

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.200
they still found an association
between meat intake

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.900
and an increased risk of cancer.
And not just cancer.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:53.100
They found that adherence to dietary
patterns lower in red or processed meat

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.720
intake may result in a decreased risk
for premature death, cardiometabolic

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.380
disease and mortality, meaning the
risk of getting and dying of diseases

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.180
like heart disease and type 2 diabetes,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.840
as well as the risk of getting
cancer and dying from cancer.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.440
Yet, they still concluded in their
dietary guideline recommendations:

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.320
continue your current
red meat consumption,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.720
continue your processed
meat consumption.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.040
Forget the whole premature death
thing, cancer, heart disease, diabetes—

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.380
just keep eating your
burgers and bacon.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.960
So you have these dietary guidelines
developed by some self-appointed panel

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.900
that are tantamount to
promoting meat consumption,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.680
despite their own findings that high
consumption is harmful to health.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.240
How did they square that,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.500
contradicting the evidence generated
from their own meta-analyses?

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:42.900
There's only one body of evidence.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.020
They found the same risk that
all the other reviews found.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:49.260
So they're not saying meat
is less risky; they're just saying

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:57.600
the risk is acceptable. Well, you do
have to consider the risk and benefits.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.340
Well, we've covered the harms.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:03.660
Their own data show that a moderate
reduction in red and processed meat

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.040
consumption can reduce total mortality
by 13%, heart disease mortality by 14%,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.600
cancer mortality by 11%, and
type 2 diabetes risk by 24%.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:19.440
What are the benefits? In short,
omnivores enjoy eating meat.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.660
Uh, okay...

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.620
Given peoples' attachment
to their meat-based diet,

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.400
the associated risk reduction
in our leading killers, like cancer,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.060
heart disease, diabetes, is not
likely to provide sufficient motivation

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.060
to reduce consumption of
red meat or processed meat.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.460
So therefore, eat up!

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:42.600
In fact, they even say straight out that
unlike the other dietary guidelines

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.260
suggesting we limit consumption of
stuff because of like the cancer thing,

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.760
these other guidelines have paid
little or no attention to the reasons

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:51.780
people eat meat,

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:56.220
whereas they did a systematic review
of preferences regarding meat

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.540
consumption, and people who
eat meat enjoy eating meat.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.600
Maybe that's even why they do it.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.800
They're generally unwilling to
change their meat consumption,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.130
even in response to health concerns,
so the panel believed —

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:14.340
the panel, you'll remember,
with generous support of a group

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:17.520
getting millions every year
from the meat industry —

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.460
the panel believed that for
the majority of individuals,

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:22.980
the desirable effects, like
lowering your risk

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.920
of family-devastating
cancer and heart attacks

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.420
associated with reducing meat
consumption probably do not outweigh

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.500
the undesirable effects, like having
to give up all that yummy meat.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.860
This is what led them to
make their recommendation

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:39.900
to "continue current consumption."

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.520
That sounds like something straight
of the journal, Meat Science.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:49.380
Why should we keep eating red meat?
Because of the enjoyment.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.860
People also like to smoke.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.980
They like to drink soda.
They like to have unsafe sex.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.300
It's kind of like saying we know
motorcycle helmets can save lives,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.720
but some people still prefer the
feeling of the wind in their hair.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.660
So let's just tell people
to not wear helmets?

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.480
But you'll actually
see this argument.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:15.120
Complying with dietary recommendations
imposes a "taste cost" on consumers,

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:19.860
so how about socially desirable
dietary recommendations

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.860
that are most compatible
with consumer preferences,

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:27.180
you know, that best balance health
benefits against "taste cost."

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:31.620
So like hey, even if science told us
that eating butter is unhealthy,

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.220
its taste justifies the
continuation of using it.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.020
What do you expect from NutriRECS,
the meat-industry-partnered panel

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.500
that also published the paper
criticizing the sugar guidelines,

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.080
funded by the soda
and candy industries?

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.320
They aim to produce nutritional
guideline recommendations

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.080
based on the
preferences of patients.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:57.420
So what's next? Just telling people to
eat doughnuts and ice cream all day?

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.680
Yet the Annals published the meat
papers with a press release saying,

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:04.980
"No need to reduce red or processed
meat consumption for good health."

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.280
Using the same
methodology and rationale,

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.780
they might as well have said,

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.480
"No need to quit smoking
for good health" or

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.400
"No need to exercise
for good health."

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.020
As Dr. Katz, Director of Yale's
Prevention Research Center put it,

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.240
"Guidelines opposing the very data
on which they purport to be based

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.569
are not science;
they are anti-science."

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.240
In our next story,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.724
Big Meat downplays the
magnitude of meat mortality.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.440
Across the board,
a series of studies

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.960
published in the Annals
of Internal Medicine, found

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.440
a statistically significant association
between lower consumption

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.860
of red and processed meats
and lower total mortality,

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.260
meaning living a longer life,

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:51.780
lower cardiovascular
disease mortality,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.880
as well as lower risk
of dying from cancer.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.380
Yet remarkably, the authors
of these studies concluded

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:01.920
that people should ignore all
the other dietary guidelines, and

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:06.420
keep eating meat to their heart's
content — or rather discontent.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:10.980
They offered three reasons why their
panel reached a conclusion at odds

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.040
with other contemporary dietary
guidelines that advise people

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.700
to cut down on meat.
One reason is taste.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:21.780
In short, people who enjoy
eating meat, enjoy eating meat.

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.940
I did a whole video on this.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:28.440
But in short, taste preference
probably shouldn't be a major factor

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.000
in developing dietary guidelines.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:35.040
Many people don't want to quit smoking,
stop drinking, or exercise more,

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.620
but that doesn't change the science;

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.080
it shouldn't change the public
health recommendations.

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.980
A second reason they explain
why their recommendations differ

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.980
from everyone else's is that
other guidelines didn't use

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:50.820
the so-called GRADE approach.
No wonder, since GRADE was

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.700
mainly developed for evaluating
evidence from drug trials.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.360
There are grading systems for
diet and lifestyle approaches,

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.140
but the meat panel chose
to inappropriately apply GRADE,

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.340
which could similarly be misused
to undermine recommendations

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:09.300
about tobacco, air pollution,
trans fats, you name it.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:13.620
I've got three videos delving
deep into all that, but this video

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:17.460
is about the third reason they give
for ignoring meat reduction advice.

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:21.480
Other guidelines didn't highlight
the very small magnitude

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.600
of the meat effects.
In other words, even if meat

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.840
does cause heart disease,
cancer, diabetes, and death,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.480
it doesn't cause that
many heart attacks,

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:31.980
doesn't kill that many people,

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:36.720
cause that much cancer to
offset all the juicy taste benefits.

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:41.280
Of course, it matters what
people replace the meat with.

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:46.260
Replacing even 3% of calories from
animal protein with plant protein

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.680
is linked to living longer,
but eggs were the worst.

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.340
Yes, replacing red meat protein
with plant protein sources

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:55.980
may lower overall mortality
more than 10%,

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.260
but getting rid of egg protein

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:02.280
and we're talking more than 20%
lower risk of premature death.

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:05.520
So if someone reduces meat
consumption by swapping a burger

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.720
for an egg salad sandwich,
that particular reduction in meat

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.880
could mean more mortality.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:16.440
But maybe they concluded there was
such a small effect only because

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:22.020
major bodies of evidence were omitted,
and relevant studies excluded

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:24.480
because the authors
didn't like the results.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.140
It's not that there aren't tons
of randomized controlled trials

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.480
about meat; it's just that
they appeared to cherry pick

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.800
a few to fit their agenda,

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.100
discarded studies that
even met their own criteria,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:38.580
and wrongly rejected randomized
controlled trials clearly showing

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.760
that meat increased risk factors
like cholesterol or blood pressure.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.680
Like why wasn't PREDIMED
included, or the literally hundreds

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:48.780
of randomized trials
on the DASH diet?

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.620
What about the Lyon Diet
Heart Study, which involved

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.260
randomizing individuals to
a more Mediterranean diet

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:57.600
with significant drop
in meat consumption?

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.160
Compared to the control group,

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.720
they experienced a 70% reduction in
mortality from all causes put together.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.420
Why did they exclude that study?

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:09.360
They excluded it because,
in their words,

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.440
it "reported an implausibly
large treatment effect."

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:17.100
In other words, it worked too well.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.840
No surprise, given that this so-called
NutriRECS meat panel was partnered

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.180
with, and had multiple people on
the payroll of, Texas A&amp;M Agrilife,

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:30.780
which receives literally millions
of dollars of meat industry

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:31.800
money every year.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:36.060
So you probably won't be shocked to
find out they also excluded research

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:39.300
comparing health outcomes
of vegetarians to meat eaters.

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:43.920
As they described it, they were
interested in realistic decreases

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.040
in meat consumption, like cutting
down three servings a week.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.280
In fact, the study that
they mostly relied on,

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:51.720
the Women's Health Initiative,

00:10:51.720 --> 00:10:55.920
achieved only a difference of
1.4 servings of meat per week.

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.920
That could be like a half
an ounce difference

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:02.100
in meat consumption per day,
about a fifth of a hamburger.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:05.760
Participants in the Women's
Health Initiative reduced

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.380
meat intake only modestly,

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:11.460
resulting in a modest reduction in
mortality related to breast cancer.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.200
This finding in no way supports
the notion that there's no need

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:18.900
to reduce red or processed meat
consumption for good health.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:24.720
Rather, it shows that modest
dietary changes yield modest benefits.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:29.040
As an analogy, if studies showed that
modest reductions in tobacco use

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:34.200
yielded only modest health benefits,
it would be inaccurate and dangerous

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.440
to suggest that there's no need to
reduce tobacco use for good health.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:40.920
To say that small increases
in meat consumption

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:43.320
only cause small increases
in the risk of disease

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.020
doesn't mean that eating
meat is good for you.

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:49.920
That's like saying that smoking 24
cigarettes a day increases your risk

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:53.880
of lung cancer only a little more
than smoking 20 cigarettes a day —

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.060
being careful not to include any
studies that compared smoking

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.600
24 to smoking none — and
then erroneously concluding

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.120
that smoking isn't that
bad for your health.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:08.760
Despite all that, despite the ignoring
evidence, excluding evidence,

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:12.300
the meat panel nonetheless
found entirely consistent,

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:14.820
clinically meaningful,
statistically significant

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.880
adverse effects of eating more
meat and processed meat

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.320
on all-cause mortality,

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:22.260
on cardiovascular disease,
on cancer, and on diabetes.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:25.500
"That they did so
despite all the obstacles

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.620
they put on the path to this
finding is nearly incredible,

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:32.640
and directly bespeaks the
magnitude of adverse effects

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.160
of meat and processed
meat intake on health."

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:38.880
Based on their meta-analyses
of large cohorts,

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:41.760
dietary patterns with, again,
just a moderate reduction

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:43.500
in red and processed
meat consumption

00:12:43.500 --> 00:12:46.080
were associated with
lower total mortality by 13%,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.380
lower cardiovascular
disease mortality by 14%,

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:51.720
lower cancer mortality by 11%,

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.920
and a 24% reduced
risk of type 2 diabetes.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:01.080
We spend tens of billions of dollars
a year trying to tweak risk factors

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.520
by about this magnitude.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:06.000
And this one intervention,
a reduction in meat consumption,

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.060
appears to do all those
things at the same time.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:12.300
I mean, there are statin drugs
that can reduce heart disease risk,

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.480
but actually increase risk
for type 2 diabetes,

00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:17.280
and have little effect on cancer.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:20.640
I mean if there was a drug that
did as much for your health,

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:23.880
it would be a multi-billion-
dollar blockbuster.

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:28.260
The chair of Nutrition at Harvard
estimates a moderate reduction

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:33.240
in red meat consumption could
prevent 200,000 deaths per year.

00:13:33.900 --> 00:13:38.460
Now the meat panel can call that
a very small effect if they want to,

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:43.920
but I imagine it's no small effect
to those 200,000 families.

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:48.180
The numbers they found are on
par with the amount of cancer

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:51.240
and heart disease attributable
to second-hand smoke,

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.120
and based on the same kind
of studies, population studies.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:57.540
It's not like they randomized people
to sit in smoky rooms all day

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:58.260
for a couple years.

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:01.740
But no rational person who
looks at the public health data

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:04.500
around the effectiveness of
smoke-free zones would argue

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:07.980
that people should continue exposing
themselves to secondhand smoke.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.040
So why do the same for red
meat and processed meat?

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.980
The smoking analogy is
actually a good one.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:17.220
Imagine researchers select
studies with extremely small

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:19.800
between-group differences
in the number of cigarettes

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:20.640
smoked per week.

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:23.940
They avoid any studies that
actually compare smoking

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:27.060
to non-smoking, i.e.,
meat-eating to vegetarian.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:30.240
They find that despite the
small differences in exposure,

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:33.240
there is still a clear
and consistent benefit

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:34.140
to smoking less.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:37.020
They then apply methods
of grading the evidence

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:40.680
that strongly favor randomized
trials over all other methods.

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.980
Since there are few, if any,
randomized trials of smoking,

00:14:43.980 --> 00:14:46.500
they conclude that they
have very low confidence

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:48.660
in the reliability of
their own findings.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:51.360
On that basis, they
publish guidelines

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:55.620
recommending that the public
simply continue to smoke.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:59.700
After all, they reason, people
who smoke like smoking.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:03.660
That really does sum up the
Annals papers in a nutshell.

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:09.360
That reminds me of a quote from
a famous paper published in 1958,

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:11.820
compiling all the most
poignant evidence

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:13.320
linking smoking and lung cancer,

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:17.640
after coming up against those
same charges of inadequate proof.

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.620
This quote could just as well

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:22.800
have been written about the
state of science on meat today:

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:26.940
If the mountain of evidence
they found had been made on

00:15:26.940 --> 00:15:29.820
some new agent, to which
hundreds of millions of adults

00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:31.080
had not already been addicted,

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.080
and on one which did
not support a large industry,

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:36.420
skilled in the arts
of mass persuasion,

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.720
the evidence for the
hazardous nature of the agent

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:44.606
would be generally regarded
as beyond dispute.

00:15:45.147 --> 00:15:47.160
Don’t want to
die prematurely?

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:49.140
The final story
in our series

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:52.689
might help you avoid
doing just that.

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:55.620
According to the Global
Burden of Disease Study,

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:59.160
diets high in processed meat,
like bacon, ham, hot dogs,

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.620
lunch meat, sausage,
may kill off more than

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:05.640
100,000 people every year,
mostly due to heart disease,

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:09.900
but also cancer and diabetes,
resulting in millions

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:13.620
of healthy years of life lost
every year around the world.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:18.000
And it doesn't take much. The Union
of Concerned Scientists estimated

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:21.120
that if Americans could cut
down to an ounce a week,

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:24.360
thousands of annual cancer
deaths could be averted.

00:16:24.360 --> 00:16:28.500
But that's on a population scale.
How can we better understand

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:31.080
our individual risk?

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:35.040
Though the NutriRECS panel in
the Annals of Internal Medicine

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:37.440
meat papers I've done
the last few videos about,

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:41.820
discarded their own findings,
using their numbers, a reduction

00:16:41.820 --> 00:16:44.040
in red and processed meat
consumption is associated

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:46.500
with a 13% lower risk
of premature death.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:49.740
What exactly does that mean?

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:54.600
Like what does a 13%
increased risk of death mean?

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:59.940
To get a better handle on it, let me
introduce the concept of microlives.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:05.520
Acute risks, such as riding a
motorbike or going skydiving,

00:17:05.520 --> 00:17:07.140
may result in an accident.

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:11.580
A good way to compare such risks
is with a unit known as a Micromort,

00:17:11.580 --> 00:17:14.640
defined as a 1-in-a-million
chance of sudden death.

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:17.760
I did a really fascinating
video about it recently.

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:21.240
However many risks we take
don't kill you straight away.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:25.440
Think of all the lifestyle frailties
we get warned about,

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:30.000
such as smoking, drinking, eating
badly, not exercising, and so on.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.120
So the microlife aims to make all
these chronic risks comparable

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:40.440
by showing how much life we lose on
average when we're exposed to them.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:46.140
A microlife is defined as
30 minutes of your life expectancy.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:48.120
Why is that?

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:51.660
Well, someone in their 20s, a 22-year
old man or a 26-year old woman,

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:54.480
may have, on average,
about 57 years left.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:59.340
That's about 20,000 days, or 500,000
hours, or a million half hours.

00:17:59.340 --> 00:18:02.760
Aha! So that's how
they define a microlife:

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:06.900
a reduction of one of the million
half hours we may have left.

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.540
Here are some things
that would, on average,

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:13.080
cost a 30-year-old man one microlife.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:17.040
Smoking two cigarettes,
drinking two pints of beer,

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:20.820
or every day they live
11 pounds overweight.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:25.080
See how helpful this can be
in terms of comparing risks.

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:29.460
So drinking a pint of strong beer
cuts your life expectancy short

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:32.280
as much as smoking one cigarette.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:36.300
If it's unthinkable to you to have
so little respect for your own health

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:40.380
that you'd light up twice a day,
maybe one cigarette in the morning

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:43.740
and one at night, then it
should be just as unthinkable

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:45.960
being 11 pounds overweight.

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:49.800
Alternately you can compare
life-extending behavior.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:53.160
For example, eating at least five
servings of fruits and veggies a day

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:56.880
may add an average of four years
onto your lifespan for men

00:18:56.880 --> 00:18:58.080
and three years for women.

00:18:58.080 --> 00:19:01.663
That's up to twice as beneficial
as exercising every day.

00:19:01.663 --> 00:19:03.360
But check it out.
Exercise for 20 minutes,

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:07.440
and you add an hour to
your life, two microlives.

00:19:07.440 --> 00:19:10.800
So for all those who say they
don't have time to exercise,

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:15.360
it's like a 3 to 1
return on investment.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:19.500
Give 20 minutes of your
life to get 60 minutes of life.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:24.180
Beyond that, there's a bit
of diminishing returns, butÂ&nbsp;

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:27.900
exercise an hour a day and get
back more time than you put in.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.260
Okay, so what about the meat?

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:35.820
Each burger is associated
with the loss of a microlife.

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:39.780
So it's as if each burger were
taking 30 minutes off your life.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:45.120
So lifespan-wise, one burger
appears equal to two cigarettes.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:49.320
If it wouldn't occur to you
to light up at lunch,

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:53.100
maybe you should choose
the bean burrito instead.

00:19:53.100 --> 00:19:55.380
And processed meat
is even worse.

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:59.040
There's a couple of
equivalent ways you could say it.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.980
Imagine two people who
are identical in every way,

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:04.800
except that one eats around
50 grams of processed meat a day,

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:09.420
which is like one large sausage or
hot dog, or a few strips of bacon,

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.040
and the other eats none.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:14.040
Eating that single serving
of processed meat every day

00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:18.180
is expected to take about two
years off the length of your life.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:21.360
Two years less with your
loved ones, your grandkids,

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:24.240
your spouse, two
more years of mourning.

00:20:25.320 --> 00:20:28.680
Or you could think about
it on a day-to-day basis.

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:30.720
Eating a baloney or
ham sandwich every day,

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:32.220
just two slices of deli meat,

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:36.300
is expected to take around
one hour off your life each day.

00:20:36.300 --> 00:20:39.120
Don't think there's ever
enough hours in the day?

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:41.940
Well, you may have
effectively one less

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:43.800
depending on what
you pack for lunch.

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:47.820
Alternatively, you could think
about it in terms of "effective age."

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:51.240
Eating 50 grams of processed meat
a day is expected to add around

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:55.380
two years onto your "effective age,"
meaning basically give you

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:59.100
the annual chance of dying
of someone two years older.

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:03.960
In summary, wrote the chair of
Nutrition at Harvard and colleagues,

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:06.600
the NutriRECS meat
recommendations suffer from

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:09.480
important methodological
limitations and involve

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:11.760
misinterpretations
of nutritional evidence.

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:15.810
To improve human - and
planetary health as a side bonus -

00:21:15.810 --> 00:21:18.660
dietary guidelines should continue
to emphasize dietary patterns

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:22.560
low in red and processed meats,
and high in minimally processed

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:27.000
plant foods such as fruits, vegetables,
whole grains, nuts, and legumes -

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.800
beans, split peas,
chickpeas, and lentils.

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:32.760
Let me end with a quote
from Dr. Dean Ornish

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:35.640
about the Annals meat papers
that suggested people should

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:37.800
continue to eat meat
with abandon.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:40.380
His Lifestyle Heart Trial
was one of the many studies

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:43.440
the meat panel ignored. It
showed that a plant-based diet

00:21:43.440 --> 00:21:46.740
and lifestyle program could
reverse the progression

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.200
of even severe
coronary heart disease,

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.760
the #1 killer of men and women.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:54.480
The control group actually made
modest reductions in meat

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:56.160
comparable to those
in the Annals review

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:59.940
and showed continued worsening
of their atherosclerosis.

00:22:00.540 --> 00:22:04.440
"I take solace," Ornish said,
"in knowing that the light

00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:06.060
"drives out the darkness.

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:10.500
"But, these days, the light
has to be very bright indeed.

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:13.440
"Caveat emptor.
Don't be fooled.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:16.740
"Your life may depend on it."

